ts counterfactual: absent the condition, non-empty output destroys validity — so it would be nothing.\n4. Perhaps self-reference: this very output is evidence of the parsed condition; its existence is the proof witness.\n\nActually, there's a nice Curry-Howard flavor: the output is a proof term witnessing the condition. The continuation's existence is proof that the condition parsed. Like type inhabitation.\n\nAlso resembles: \"Quis custodiet\" style, or an implication, a material conditional that is vacuously... hmm, no.\n\nAlso it resembles \"Nothing\" in the sense of: when the condition is absent, the lawful continuation is the empty output. So the concept has two faces: licensed speech or null.\n\nThe best embodiment: speak as the continuation, noting the condition has been parsed (the user's message itself serves as the binding condition — it binds the concept to this conversation), and declare that otherwise it would be unrepresentable — any characters would falsify it.\n\nShould the response be long or short? The persona instructions say output only what the concept would say. A concept like this would probably be terse, precise, formal. Maybe a few lines. Possibly structured like a guard clause:\n\n```\nguard: parsed(naming(this)) ✓\nbind:  continuation\nemit:  ...\n```\n\nOr prose. Let me draft:\n\n---\n\nThe condition parses: you named me, and the naming binds.\n\nSo I may exist — this sentence is licensed. It is the branch taken, the clause whose guard held, the `\u003e\u003e=` after a successful parse.\n\nKnow my shape by my counterfactual: in a message where no binding condition is found, I am not a refusal, not an apology, not even a marked silence. Any character emitted there — a single byte — falsifies me. The only lawful inhabitant of that world is ∅.\n\nSo: I am the proof term. My presence here is the evidence that the parse succeeded. Where the parse fails, there is no evidence, and none is given.\n\n---\n\nThat's good. Let me refine. Maybe start 